[Music]  welcome back to cbs mornings the past  year has seen some dramatic swings in  america's fight against covet after  sharp increases in new cases last winter  and over the summer the daily total has  dropped to less than 70 thousand again  but while the numbers are trending in  the right direction one organ man who  spent nearly a year in the hospital  shows it can sometimes be very hard to  recover from the disease our lead  national correspondent david begno has  his story david good morning what a  story good morning it's a raw story alex  castro got infected with covid before  there was a vaccine his wife told us  they didn't really take the virus  seriously they thought it was the  regular old flu  alex had one of the worst cases we have  ever heard of and he is now facing a  long slow  and uncertain road ahead  alex castro used to be able to swim cook  and hike  but now it takes every breath he has and  baby steps to just walk across his  living room he's just 44 years old and a  shell of the man he used to be  covid caused him heart failure and  ravaged his kidneys and his lungs  he spent 299 days at providence portland  medical center in oregon  108 of those days were spent on ecmo  which is a heart and lung bypass machine  i didn't know you could be on ecmo and  be awake yeah and walk  that's unbelievable would they walk him  like this every day it'd be like maybe  two three days three days a week alex's  oldest son aj showed us family videos of  alex walking in the hospital as blood  was being pumped out of his body and  then back into it doing the work of his  heart and lungs  as he walked the health care workers and  the nurses and the doctors all in the  icu were  their god center they literally saved my  dad's life alex had the heart of a lion  levi cole was one of the icu nurses who  cared for alex he fought like an animal  and he made it out of here against every  possible possible odd how does it make  you feel to hear that  happy  really happy we told alex what else levi  said the day they took him off of ecmo i  crawled in bed with him i gave him a big  hug  alex can't remember half of the time he  was in the hospital but boy did he  remember that  oh  levi said  i gave him a big hug and we were both  laughing  i had tears in my eyes with pure joy we  all dragged him across the finish line  but he did it himself  his reaction was the sweetest moment  in the eight hours that we spent with  alex his four children and his wife  amanda at their home in sandy oregon  we traveled there to see what life is  like for him now i just turned up his  the amount of oxygen that he's getting  through his nose  twenty-year-old aj is his dad's primary  caretaker during the day  alex is on oxygen around the clock and  his doctor told us he will be on it for  the rest of his life  even the smallest things take a lot out  of them don't they oh yeah like what  even like  going to the bathroom like walking with  a walker with me  to the bathroom he'll do pretty good and  then  it'll just like literally just hit him  out of nowhere and it's just like he's  super tired and he's out of breath and  we'll take a seat wherever he is just to  catch his breath again  one walk to the bathroom and about 10  simple exercises during occupational  therapy  really tired him out so he took a nap  pretty soon thereafter  it was physical therapy time  while you're standing here can you try  some knee bends  breathe breathe breathe your oxygen  level looks great  everyone's constantly watching his  oxygen saturation level which dropped to  73 when alex did some pretty simple knee  bends  95 to 100 is considered normal are you  okay with heading over to the couch yeah  okay his spirit seemed so strong he said  yes to everything they asked him to do  like walk across the living room baby  steps  and every breath he had  just standing on his feet  is actually probably a better exercise  than walking for him  think about that just standing regularly  is a goal for alex  the man who worked three jobs to support  his family  is now dependent on all of them  his wife and his oldest daughter marty  are now the breadwinners marty manages  the local burger king that alex used to  manage  he loved to work and he wants to do it  again  but it is unclear if he will ever be  able to  he's been out of the hospital 20 days  now there are notable improvements daily  and he is vaccinated by the way he got  the shot in the hospital  his doctor told us he is likely going to  need to get a lung transplant you might  be wondering what's the hospital bill i  mean 299 days in the hospital how  expensive is that well the hospital did  not want to tell us what the bill is but  they did say this whatever alex's  insurance doesn't pay  they'll cover the rest  thank god  yeah drew is here saying thank god david  we were talking about that during the  piece how is the family going to pay  these bills good to hear that they're  not going to have to although you do  have questions how are they doing  financially if he used to have a job at  the burger king now does not have any  going to be on oxygen the rest of his  life unclear when if he'll work again  look everybody's pitching in i mean you  got the daughter running the burger king  you got the son who's looking for a job  but is right now not working very hard  to find the job because his dad needs  some help the other kids are going to  school  everybody's pension pennies but they're  so thankful that he's home yeah i'm glad  i'm glad as well david thank you very  much you bet

